---
// MIRA reference.
//
// This is a reference page, not a working document. It states what the schema requires, how
// this corpus maps onto it, and what is known to be wrong upstream. The mapping tables and
// every figure come from corpus-facts.json, generated from the exporter's own tables and the
// export files, so the page cannot drift from what the code does.
//
// The project's working notes on MIRA — how each of these findings was arrived at — stay in
// docs/schema-mapping/mira-guide.md and are linked at the foot of the page.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StandardsTabs from '../../components/StandardsTabs.astro';
import { byId as stdById } from '../../lib/standards';
import { readFileSync } from 'fs';
import { join } from 'path';

const facts = JSON.parse(readFileSync(
  join(process.cwd(), 'src/data/corpus-facts.json'), 'utf8'));
const m = facts.mira;
const map = facts.mira_mapping;

const CORE = 'mira:supports';
const supports = map.relations.filter((r: any) => r.declared_under === 'mira:supports');
const opposes  = map.relations.filter((r: any) => r.declared_under === 'mira:opposes');
const neutral  = map.relations.filter((r: any) => !r.declared_under);

const roleRows = Object.entries(map.roles) as [string, string][];
const short = (s: string) => s.replace(/^mira:|^dg:/, '');

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const std = stdById['mira'];
const val = JSON.parse(readFileSync(
  join(process.cwd(), 'src/data/mira-validation.json'), 'utf8'));
const byViolations = Object.entries(val.papers as Record<string, any>)
  .sort((a, b) => b[1].upstream - a[1].upstream);
---

<Layout title="MIRA reference">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">

<StandardsTabs current="mira" />

    <header class="mb-9">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 tracking-tight m-0 mb-2">MIRA</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 m-0 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        {std.full} — {std.blurb} This states what we believe the schema requires and how a claim
        tree maps onto it. Where we have read it wrong, the last section is where to tell us.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-3 m-0">
        Pinned at <code>{m.pinned_schema_commit?.slice(0, 8)}</code> ·
        mapping tables generated from <code>scripts/export_mira.py</code> ·
        <a href={`${base}/pipeline/mira-export/`} class="text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 no-underline hover:underline">the export layer, per paper</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Figures -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-3 mb-12">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800/50 rounded-xl p-4">
        <div class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">{m.carried}</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-1">of {m.relations} relations carried</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800/50 rounded-xl p-4">
        <div class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">{m.lost}</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-1">not representable</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800/50 rounded-xl p-4">
        <div class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">{m.our_violations}</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-1">validation violations from this encoding</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800/50 rounded-xl p-4">
        <div class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">{m.synthesised_questions}</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-1">Questions synthesised, each flagged</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The schema -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mb-3">What the schema requires</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      <p>
        MIRA defines fourteen classes and thirty properties. For a claim graph the load-bearing
        ones are the node types &mdash; <code>Claim</code>, <code>Evidence</code>,
        <code>Question</code>, <code>Study</code>, <code>Protocol</code>,
        <code>SourceDocument</code> &mdash; and three classes imported from a Discourse Graphs
        base schema that govern relations: <code>AbstractRelationDef</code>,
        <code>RelationDef</code> and <code>RelationInstance</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Relations are reified.</strong> An edge is a node carrying <code>source</code>
        and <code>destination</code>, typed by a relation declaration. It is not a predicate on
        a claim: <code>supports</code> and <code>opposes</code> sit on an <code>Argument</code>
        mixin that neither <code>Claim</code> nor <code>Evidence</code> mixes in, and every
        generated node shape is <code>sh:closed</code>, so an inline relation is rejected.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The vocabulary is extensible.</strong> <code>AbstractRelationDef</code> names a
        relation type; <code>RelationDef</code> binds its domain and range. The abstract root
        carries no supporting or opposing commitment, which is what allows a relation that is
        neither &mdash; a boundary condition, a deductive step &mdash; to be declared rather
        than forced into <code>supports</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Every Claim addresses a Question.</strong> A claim tree has no such node, so one
        is derived from each hypothesis and flagged <code>haak:autoDerived</code>; the gap
        report lists all {m.synthesised_questions} for review.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
        MIRA is a draft — <code>bibo:status status:draft</code>, no tags or releases. This
        corpus pins commit <code>{m.pinned_schema_commit?.slice(0, 12)}</code>, vendored under
        <code>vendor/</code> so validation is reproducible offline. Note that the JSON-LD
        context is served from <code>purl.org/mira-science/mira.jsonld</code>; the GitHub Pages
        copy is stale and retypes <code>Study</code> as <code>Protocol</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Role mapping -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mt-10 mb-3">Claim roles to MIRA node types</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Claim role</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">MIRA type</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {roleRows.map(([role, type]) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-800">
              <td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>{role}</code></td>
              <td class="py-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"><code>{type}</code></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Relation mapping -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mt-10 mb-3">Relation types</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-5">
      Each is declared in every export document as an <code>AbstractRelationDef</code> with a
      domain, a range and a description, then used to type edge nodes. Those declared under a
      core predicate remain legible to a reader who follows only <code>supports</code> and
      <code>opposes</code>.
    </p>

    {[['Declared under mira:supports', supports],
      ['Declared under mira:opposes', opposes],
      ['Declared neutrally — neither support nor opposition', neutral]].map(([heading, rows]: any) => (
      <div class="mb-8">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mb-2">{heading}</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Relation</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold whitespace-nowrap">Domain &rarr; range</th>
                <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Meaning</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {rows.map((r: any) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-800 align-top">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">
                    <code>{r.relation}</code>
                    {r.inverse_of && (
                      <span class="block text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-0.5">
                        inverse of <code>{r.inverse_of}</code>
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-xs">
                    {short(r.domain)} &rarr; {short(r.range)}
                  </td>
                  <td class="py-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">{r.description}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}

    <!-- Limits -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mt-10 mb-3">What the conversion does not carry</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      <p>
        <strong>Paper-level scope.</strong> {m.lost} relations of {m.relations} target the paper
        as a whole rather than another claim. <code>scopes</code> has <code>mira:Claim</code> as
        its range and MIRA has no paper-level node, so no edge is emitted and no target is
        invented. Each paper&rsquo;s gap report names them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Inverse relations.</strong> {m.inverse_only} <code>derived-from</code> relations
        are declared with <code>owl:inverseOf</code> and emitted in one direction only, which is
        MIRA&rsquo;s own practice. Both directions are recoverable from the forward edge and the
        declaration; nothing is lost.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Verification.</strong> {m.verification_records} records state which code was
        run, against which deposited file, at which commit, and what value came out beside the
        published one. MIRA has no node type for any of it &mdash; <code>observationBase</code>
        records what a claim rests on, not whether anyone recomputed it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stance.</strong> MIRA cannot record that a paper considered a proposition and
        rejected it. An alternative explanation exported as a <code>mira:Claim</code> therefore
        reads as one the paper asserts; the <code>rules-out</code> edge gives a reader the
        direction of the argument but not the paper&rsquo;s position.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both of the last two are additive &mdash; neither requires changing anything MIRA
        already does &mdash; and both travel in the extended export under a
        <code>haak:</code> namespace pending a decision upstream.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Validation -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mt-10 mb-3">Does it validate</h2>

    {/* The two numbers side by side, because the split is the finding. A single "165
        violations" would read as our failure; a single "0" would hide a real bug in a schema
        eLife depends on. */}
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 border border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-600 rounded-lg overflow-hidden mb-5">
      <div class="p-5 border-b sm:border-b-0 sm:border-r border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-600">
        <div class="text-4xl font-mono font-medium text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 tabular-nums leading-none">{m.our_violations}</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mt-2 m-0 leading-snug">
          violations attributable to <strong>our encoding</strong>, across all
          {' '}{byViolations.length} exports, against the pinned shapes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-5">
        <div class="text-4xl font-mono font-medium text-red-700 dark:text-red-400 tabular-nums leading-none">{m.upstream_violations}</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mt-2 m-0 leading-snug">
          from <strong>one bug in MIRA&rsquo;s own generated shapes</strong>, on two properties,
          which no document can satisfy.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="border border-red-300 dark:border-red-800/70 bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-950/30 rounded-lg p-5 mb-6">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 m-0 mb-2">
        A four-node document, using only MIRA&rsquo;s own terms, that MIRA&rsquo;s own shapes reject
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 m-0 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        {val.upstream_bug?.summary}
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-900/60 border border-red-200 dark:border-red-900/60 rounded p-3 text-xs overflow-x-auto m-0"><code>pyshacl -s vendor/mira.shacl -sf turtle -e vendor/mira.ttl \
        -df json-ld docs/schema-mapping/mira-sh-in-bug.jsonld

Conforms: False &middot; {val.upstream_bug?.proof_violations} violations, one per property</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 rounded-lg mb-3">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
            <th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-medium">Paper</th>
            <th class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-right">Ours</th>
            <th class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-right">Upstream</th>
            <th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-medium">Failing property</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {byViolations.map(([slug, d]: [string, any]) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-800 last:border-0">
              <td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-xs text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">{slug}</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">{d.ours}</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">{d.upstream}</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-[11px] text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">{d.paths.map((x: string) => x.replace('mira:', '')).join(', ')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-6">
      Generated by <code>scripts/validate_mira.py</code> against <code>vendor/mira.shacl</code> at <code>{val.pinned_schema_commit?.slice(0, 8)}</code>
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      <p>
        Against the pinned shapes, these exports produce <strong>{m.our_violations} violations
        attributable to this encoding</strong>. The {m.upstream_violations} that remain come
        from one constraint in MIRA&rsquo;s generated shapes that no document can satisfy:
        <code>gen-shacl</code> renders <code>mira:addresses</code> and
        <code>mira:sourceDocument</code> with an <code>sh:in</code> list of slot names beside an
        <code>sh:class</code> and <code>sh:nodeKind sh:BlankNodeOrIRI</code>, so each shape
        requires an IRI and then requires that IRI to be one of two strings.
      </p>
      <p>
        A four-node document using only MIRA&rsquo;s own terms fails identically. It ships as
        <code>docs/schema-mapping/mira-sh-in-bug.jsonld</code> so the distinction between a
        schema fault and an encoding fault can be checked rather than asserted.
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800/50 rounded-lg p-4 text-xs overflow-x-auto"><code>python3 scripts/validate_mira.py</code></pre>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
        The script reproduces the upstream fault first and refuses to classify anything if that
        proof stops behaving as documented, then splits every violation into upstream or ours
        and exits non-zero if any is ours.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Open questions -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mt-10 mb-3">Open questions for the MIRA authors</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 list-disc pl-5">
      <li>Is the reified relation node the intended encoding? It is derivable only from
        closed-shape failures, and a line in the README would save every consumer the work.</li>
      <li>Should <code>Claim</code> and <code>Evidence</code> mix in <code>Argument</code>? They
        do in <code>discoursegraphs.yaml</code> and <code>mira.ttl</code> but not in
        <code>mira.yaml</code>, which decides whether an inline <code>supports</code> is ever
        legal.</li>
      <li>Is the <code>sh:in</code> artifact a known issue in the forked LinkML generator?</li>
      <li><code>subClassOf</code> is typed <code>@id</code> rather than <code>@vocab</code>, so
        bare terms in the reference data resolve against the document base rather than the
        vocabulary. Intended?</li>
      <li><code>sh:closed true</code> on every node shape leaves no sanctioned extension point.
        Is one planned?</li>
      <li>Would MIRA accept a <code>Verification</code> node type, carrying the code, the data
        identity, the reproduced value and the published value?</li>
    </ul>

    <footer class="mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-800 text-sm flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
      <a href={`${base}/standards/`} class="text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-300 no-underline">&larr; Standards</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/MIRA-science/schema" target="_blank"
         class="text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-300 no-underline">MIRA schema &nearr;</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/zmainen/elife-claim-trees/blob/main/scripts/export_mira.py" target="_blank"
         class="text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-300 no-underline">Exporter source &nearr;</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/zmainen/elife-claim-trees/blob/main/docs/schema-mapping/mira-guide.md" target="_blank"
         class="text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-300 no-underline">Working notes &nearr;</a>
    </footer>
  </div>
</Layout>
